WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.500 --> 00:00:11.900
The first input controls we'll talk about today are the checkbox and the radio button. We probably all know them and use them a lot, but there are some guidelines to go through.

2
00:00:11.900 --> 00:00:24.100
First point: when do I use a radio button and when a checkbox? Radio buttons are for options that are mutually exclusive: I can't pick more than one at the same time, and

3
00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:36.300
picking one means I can't pick another. In this example, if I choose a type of burger I can't choose more than one type, I need exactly one, so I use a radio button.

4
00:00:36.300 --> 00:00:47.950
Unlike a checkbox, which I use when the user can pick more than one option, or none at all. In the example, if I want to add

5
00:00:47.950 --> 00:00:59.600
things to the burger I can add cheese and tomato, I can add all three together, or add nothing. Here a checkbox fits best. That's the first guideline.

6
00:00:59.600 --> 00:01:08.700
Also important, especially on desktop: when I have a single checkbox like this, I can use it to turn something on or off. Like the

7
00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:17.800
example we had earlier in Figma, fixing an element's position while scrolling. I turn this checkbox on to enable that feature, and it's common on desktop.

8
00:01:17.800 --> 00:01:27.367
The second guideline we can find in this example. At first glance these are checkboxes stacked under each other. But if we tap

9
00:01:27.367 --> 00:01:36.933
one, it starts to look like a button. So is it a checkbox or a button? We might get confused. If we tap another,

10
00:01:36.933 --> 00:01:46.500
we find it's selected along with the first, so more than one choice: it's a checkbox, but its look is a mix of both.

11
00:01:46.500 --> 00:01:56.700
From here we get the guideline that says to use a standard visual representation. This is an important idea that keeps coming

12
00:01:56.700 --> 00:02:06.900
back with design patterns: we use them because users already know them and use them easily without learning anything new. If I

13
00:02:06.900 --> 00:02:17.100
change the component's look, the user may not recognize it as one they know, and I've lost the benefit of the design pattern.

14
00:02:17.100 --> 00:02:26.850
So we use the standard look: a checkbox is a square with a tick inside it, or even an X,

15
00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:36.600
but a circle is always a radio. And a radio button is a circle with a filled circle inside it.

16
00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:47.067
The next guideline we learn from this example. If we see radio buttons like this we may get confused: I

17
00:02:47.067 --> 00:02:57.533
want to choose 4, do I tap the radio after the number or before it? I'd have to go back

18
00:02:57.533 --> 00:03:08.000
to the start and discover that each radio belongs to the word after it. The problem is that we put

19
00:03:08.000 --> 00:03:18.467
the options side by side with no space, so it isn't clear which radio belongs to which option. The guideline

20
00:03:18.467 --> 00:03:28.933
says: it's always better to stack radio buttons or checkboxes under each other if there's space. If we must place

21
00:03:28.933 --> 00:03:39.400
them side by side, leave enough space so we can tell each radio belongs to the option next to it.

22
00:03:39.400 --> 00:03:49.850
The last guideline: in this example the options are all worded as negatives, I don't want cheese, I don't

23
00:03:49.850 --> 00:04:00.300
want tomato. So we use positive and active wording, so the user can tell the choice apart easily. If I

24
00:04:00.300 --> 00:04:10.750
tell them I won't add cheese and they tick it, they get confused: am I ticking to add cheese

25
00:04:10.750 --> 00:04:21.200
or to not add it? So we always use the affirmative, not the negative, so the choice is always easier.
